extra_foundations = """
          <p>The perceptual science governing volumetric area charts is intimately linked to the gestalt principles of visual perception, specifically the laws of common fate, continuity, and figure-ground segregation. When an observer examines a continuous filled area polygon, the human visual cortex processes the enclosed region as a unified visual object possessing mass, inertia, and physical substance. In contrast to disconnected line segments that require active mental synthesis to deduce cumulative trends, an area chart provides direct optical encoding of aggregate volume, enabling executive stakeholders to perform split-second volumetric estimations.</p>
          <p>However, stacked area visualizations introduce a well-documented perceptual vulnerability known as baseline-induced distortion. In a classic 2010 study conducted by Jeffrey Heer, Michael Bostock, and Vadim Ogievetsky at Stanford University, human subjects evaluated part-to-whole estimation errors across various chart geometries. The study proved that while the bottom-most series (anchored to a stable zero baseline) is decoded with high precision, intermediate stacked layers exhibit significantly higher estimation error rates. Because the bottom boundary of intermediate layers undulates in response to preceding series fluctuations, viewers struggle to isolate the layer's true vertical thickness from its floating baseline altitude.</p>
          <p>To mitigate baseline-induced distortion, data architects must choose between two complementary visualization doctrines: when absolute scalar accuracy across individual series is mandatory, small multiples (separate small area charts arranged in a grid with shared axes) or interactive layer isolation should be prioritized. Conversely, when the analytical goal is communicating macro-scale volumetric ebb and flow, total cluster load, and relative compositional dominance across time, the continuous stacked streamgraph represents the most evocative and holistic medium available in modern web engineering.</p>
"""

extra_baseline = """
          <p>To fully appreciate the mathematical ingenuity of Lee Byron and Martin Wattenberg's algorithm, consider the underlying optimization objective. Let each layer i have thickness f_i(t) &gt; 0 and midpoint trajectory m_i(t) = y_0(t) + sum_{j=0}^{i-1} f_j(t) + 0.5 * f_i(t). In an unweighted system, the visual wiggle of layer i corresponds to the square of its midpoint slope: (m'_i(t))^2. If we simply minimized the unweighted sum of squared midpoint slopes, a tiny, volatile stream carrying 0.1% of total volume could exert an outsized pull on the baseline, forcing massive, high-volume layers to undulate wildly to accommodate the micro-stream.</p>
          <p>Byron and Wattenberg solved this asymmetry by weighting each layer's squared slope directly by its local thickness f_i(t). The global optimization functional integrates the weighted energy: E = integral sum_{i=0}^{M-1} f_i(t) * (m'_i(t))^2 dt. Differentiating this energy functional with respect to the baseline derivative y'_0(t) and setting the derivative to zero yields the exact closed-form solution: y'_0(t) = - sum_{i=0}^{M-1} (1/2 + g_{i-1}(t) / g_{M-1}(t)) * f'_i(t). This elegant result proves that the optimal baseline change at any instant is a weighted linear combination of the individual series rates of change.</p>
          <p>In discrete algorithmic implementations, the temporal derivatives f'_i(t) are computed via central finite differences: f'_i(t) = (f_i(t+1) - f_i(t-1)) / (2 * delta_t) for interior points, with forward differences (f_i(1) - f_i(0)) / delta_t at the left boundary and backward differences at the right boundary. The baseline offset y_0(t) is then obtained through numerical integration (trapezoidal accumulation). Subtracting the mean offset centers the resulting streamgraph vertically on the canvas, creating the iconic balanced river silhouette.</p>
"""

extra_spline = """
          <p>The mathematical hazard of unconstrained cubic splines is an unavoidable consequence of polynomial curve fitting, historically formalized as Runge's phenomenon. When higher-order polynomials or unconstrained cubic splines interpolate discrete points that feature sharp step-functions or sudden impulses, the interpolating polynomial must oscillate vigorously between sample points to satisfy continuous second-derivative smoothness constraints. In an SVG stacked area chart, these oscillations manifest as phantom valleys that dip below the true data points or exaggerated peaks that soar far above actual recorded metrics.</p>
          <p>The Fritsch-Carlson algorithm resolves this defect through a rigorous five-step mathematical procedure: first, calculate the secant slopes Delta_k = (y_{k+1} - y_k) / (x_{k+1} - x_k) for each interval; second, initialize internal tangent derivatives d_k as the average of adjacent secant slopes; third, check for zero-slope flatlines where Delta_k = 0, clamping d_k = d_{k+1} = 0; fourth, evaluate the directional ratios alpha_k = d_k / Delta_k and beta_k = d_{k+1} / Delta_k; fifth, if alpha_k^2 + beta_k^2 &gt; 9, constrain the vector to a circular boundary of radius 3 by scaling d_k = (3 / sqrt(alpha_k^2 + beta_k^2)) * alpha_k * Delta_k.</p>
          <p>By ensuring that the tangent slopes never exceed three times the secant slope, the Fritsch-Carlson condition guarantees that the resulting cubic Bézier curve is strictly monotonic across every monotonic interval. If the underlying data points rise from 10 to 50, the SVG path is mathematically prohibited from exceeding 50 or dropping below 10 within that span. In production financial and infrastructure telemetry dashboards, this algorithmic constraint is indispensable: it prevents misleading visual artifacts that could prompt false operational alerts or erroneous capacity planning decisions.</p>
"""

extra_painting = """
          <p>When rendering adjacent SVG path polygons with shared mathematical boundary edges, graphics engines frequently suffer from a subtle rendering bug known as the vector seam artifact. Because modern browser rasterizers apply anti-aliasing along the boundary contours of each polygon independently, the anti-aliased semi-transparent pixels along the bottom edge of layer A do not mathematically sum to 100% opacity with the semi-transparent pixels along the top edge of layer B. As a result, the dark background canvas bleeds through as a faint, jagged 1-pixel hairline crack along the interface between stacked layers.</p>
          <p>To completely eradicate vector seam cracks in production SVG streamgraphs, data visualization engineers utilize three proven techniques: first, expanding the stroke width of each layer path to 1.5 pixels using the exact same fill color, allowing the stroke border to bridge the sub-pixel rasterization gap; second, ordering path elements with a fractional 0.5-pixel vertical overlap along the shared boundary; third, applying the CSS property shape-rendering: geometricPrecision on parent container groups while declaring fill-rule: nonzero. These adjustments guarantee that contiguous volumetric layers form a cohesive, unbroken visual continuum.</p>
          <p>In addition to linear gradients, advanced streamgraphs leverage dynamic SVG pattern servers to encode auxiliary qualitative dimensions. For example, a microservice stream experiencing elevated error rates can dynamically transition from a smooth solid fill to an animated SVG striped pattern (&lt;pattern&gt; with diagonal stroke lines). Because SVG patterns are rendered purely through vector primitives, they scale crisply with zero raster pixelation, alerting operators to qualitative state changes without altering the underlying volumetric geometry.</p>
"""

extra_engine = """
          <p>For mission-critical telemetry consoles that monitor high-frequency metrics across hundreds of nodes, memory management and DOM stability are paramount. Naive implementations that wipe and rebuild the entire SVG tree via innerHTML on every update incur severe CPU penalties: the browser must tear down existing DOM trees, perform full CSS style recalculations, rebuild render trees, and trigger expensive composite passes. At 60 or 120 FPS, this garbage collection thrashing quickly freezes user interactions.</p>
          <p>A production-ready ES6 engine circumvents DOM thrashing through structural node pooling and attribute memoization. During initialization, the engine creates a fixed pool of SVG &lt;path&gt; elements matching the maximum anticipated number of streams. When new time-series data arrives over WebSocket connections, the engine updates the d attribute strings in-place using direct DOM references. Furthermore, by wrapping path coordinate updates in window.requestAnimationFrame callbacks, the engine synchronizes geometric recalculations directly with the client display refresh cycle, delivering silky-smooth 120 FPS transitions even on resource-constrained mobile devices.</p>
"""

